        {
          "id": "e30db905-86e2-384e-9371-4efd8d80255a",
          "content": {
            "id": "e30db905-86e2-384e-9371-4efd8d80255a",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "LVMH shares dive after results cast doubt over luxury recovery",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: LVMH shares plunged as much as 8% on Wednesday (January 28).</p><p>Investors sold off after its fourth-quarter results lowered hopes of a recovery in luxury demand.</p><p>The sector continued to be weighed down by slow sales in the important Chinese market.</p><p>While LVMH CEO Bernard Arnault also gave a cautious outlook.</p><p>The world's biggest luxury conglomerate is home to brands like Louis Vuitton, Tiffany and Dior.</p><p>It's seen by investors as a key marker for the wider industry's health.</p><p>Other luxury giants like Gucci owner Kering and Hermes were also dragged down Wednesday after the LVMH update.</p><p>The French group said sales in China were up in the quarter.</p><p>But investors had expected more after upbeat comments on the country from other luxury firms this month.</p><p>Market watchers estimate Chinese shoppers make up for almost a third of LVMH's fashion and leather sales.</p><p>Asia, excluding Japan, made up just over a quarter of LVMH revenues last year.</p><p>Sales in the region were up just 1% in the fourth quarter.</p><p>Sales in LVMH's key fashion and leather goods division fell 3% in currency-adjusted terms in the same quarter.</p><p>That sticks out as it happened during an important shopping period.</p><p>One analyst said investors had hoped the division would beat expectations.</p><p>Looking ahead, LVMH said Tuesday ongoing geopolitical concerns and economic uncertainty clouded their outlook.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: LVMH shares plunged as much as 8% on Wednesday (January 28).</p><p>Investors sold off after its fourth-quarter results lowered hopes of a recovery in luxury demand.</p><p>The sector continued to be weighed down by slow sales in the important Chinese market.</p><p>While LVMH CEO Bernard Arnault also gave a cautious outlook.</p><p>The world's biggest luxury conglomerate is home to brands like Louis Vuitton, Tiffany and Dior.</p><p>It's seen by investors as a key marker for the wider industry's health.</p><p>Other luxury giants like Gucci owner Kering and Hermes were also dragged down Wednesday after the LVMH update.</p><p>The French group said sales in China were up in the quarter.</p><p>But investors had expected more after upbeat comments on the country from other luxury firms this month.</p><p>Market watchers estimate Chinese shoppers make up for almost a third of LVMH's fashion and leather sales.</p><p>Asia, excluding Japan, made up just over a quarter of LVMH revenues last year.</p><p>Sales in the region were up just 1% in the fourth quarter.</p><p>Sales in LVMH's key fashion and leather goods division fell 3% in currency-adjusted terms in the same quarter.</p><p>That sticks out as it happened during an important shopping period.</p><p>One analyst said investors had hoped the division would beat expectations.</p><p>Looking ahead, LVMH said Tuesday ongoing geopolitical concerns and economic uncertainty clouded their outlook.</p></body>",
